-> DEPENDS_ON [node:822d1381-09c3-41bc-ba5c-87c90e92ef78] @ 2026-09-08T18:56:17.098001+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:ccc18558-7181-44df-9608-de587d8d937c] @ 2026-09-08T18:56:16.716439+00:00

Open-Meteo labels its timestamps iso8601 but returns naive local wall-clock strings with no offset and no Z, such as 2026-09-08T20:30, while the actual offset arrives separately as utc_offset_seconds. Passing those strings to Date interprets them in the server's zone, which is correct by accident whenever the server and the location agree and wrong the rest of the time, including twice a year at a daylight-saving boundary. Every timestamp is therefore resolved through the offset before it leaves the client. This is not tidiness: sunrise and sunset from this response are what a schedule-driven night mode keys off, and an hours-wrong sunset is a kitchen that dims at the wrong time in a way that looks plausible. The response also returns is_day as 0 or 1, and Boolean of the string zero is true.
---
