[node:bd8d7ed5-1440-4f3f-a78d-76f762696176]
type: decision
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/faza-1-is-sliced-theme-loader-first-beca
created_at: 2026-09-09T06:38:00.083354+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:c62c070b-e4a6-41b3-86f3-00ad5d96800f] @ 2026-09-09T06:38:00.083354+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-09T06:38:00.083354+00:00

Faza 1 is sliced theme-loader-first because the loader changes how every component receives its values: building more components before it means building them twice. It is also the only slice that converts an existing lifetime constraint from a claim into a demonstration - the second theme file was written so that "swapping the design is pointing theme at a different file" would be testable rather than aspirational, and until the loader exists it has never been loaded once. The calendar week view and the component stories both depend on the loader only in the sense that they should be written against the mechanism that will ship, not the direct stylesheet import that precedes it.
---
[node:8a7dfeec-017f-4639-8c7b-675caa8ab375]
type: constraint
tier: mid-term
path: /artifact/the-token-sheet-is-generated-from-the-th
created_at: 2026-09-09T06:41:30.680586+00:00
needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-09T06:41:30.680586+00:00
-> ABOUT [node:73fb6132-40d3-4e2d-8b88-334442c4bfa7] @ 2026-09-09T06:41:30.680586+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:79662ce5-7b55-458a-b0e9-f0efd406d5a8] @ 2026-09-09T06:41:30.811903+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:e12ca220-5f82-4226-a2eb-9964701f03c4] @ 2026-09-09T06:41:30.680586+00:00

The token sheet is generated from the theme file the layout names and served by the backend, never imported into the client bundle. Importing it made the layout's theme key decorative: the contract said swapping the whole design was a matter of pointing that key at another file, and nothing executed that path, so a second theme written expressly to prove it had never been loaded. Generation is checked against the committed stylesheet - colours, type scale, shape and spacing must match - so moving from a hand-written sheet to a generated one cannot quietly change the design. The sheet is served from the root and not under the API prefix because it is shell rather than data: the service worker keeps a copy so an offline paint has its colours, and fetches it network-first so a theme change appears on the next load rather than after a worker update.
---
