needs_review: false
retrieval_weight: 1.0
trust_weight: 1.0
archived: false
-> ABOUT [node:15863232-0fb0-4c79-aaa9-3750ce8c0a2f] @ 2026-09-09T12:06:31.797646+00:00
-> DEPENDS_ON [node:4a749051-6bc6-4807-bc52-47748e2bb914] @ 2026-09-09T12:06:31.797646+00:00
-> HAS_FACET [node:4c740d82-541d-4c70-9970-793467c4f26e] @ 2026-09-09T12:06:31.797646+00:00

extractFallback's Readability-based strategy initially returned a "successful" extraction (title only, empty ingredients/steps) for any page Readability considers article-shaped, even one with no list-shaped content at all — confirmed live against https://www.example.com during manual smoke testing, which is exactly the kind of page this would silently mis-handle. Fixed by treating a zero-length ingredients list as a failed extraction (return undefined), not a thin one: a real recipe page always has at least an ingredients list, so a title-only match is the fallback heuristic's version of "no honest answer" and must not be presented as if it had found a recipe. This is the same "no placeholder data as real" reasoning as node 4a749051, applied to what "successful extraction" is allowed to mean.
---
